Storing LightBTS in git
-----------------------

A LightBTS instance can be stored in the git repository of the project it
tracks, so that the tickets are shared with everyone who clones the
repository, and can be synchronized with `git push` and `git pull`.

## Why not just commit .lightbts?

Committing the `.lightbts` directory in the project's own branches would mix
the tickets with the history of the code:

- Each branch would have its own version of the tickets. A ticket created on
  a feature branch would only exist on that branch, and switching branches
  would make tickets appear and disappear.
- Merging branches would merge tickets, at times that have nothing to do with
  the tickets.
- Every change to a ticket would be a commit in the history of the code.

Tickets are not tied to the code that way: they are opened and closed at other
times than the commits that relate to them, and they are about all branches.

## The lightbts branch

Instead, the tickets are stored in a branch named `lightbts`, which shares no
history with the other branches of the repository: it is an orphan branch.
This branch is checked out as a second worktree in `.lightbts`, in the
directory of the main worktree. The main worktree ignores `.lightbts`, through
`.git/info/exclude`, so the project's own `.gitignore` doesn't need to know
about it.

The branch is a normal branch, so `git push origin lightbts` and
`git fetch` work without extra configuration, and clones get it by default.

## What is tracked

The branch contains:

- `messages/`, with all messages. These are the source of truth.
- `config`, the shared configuration: the field definitions, the project name,
  the web interface settings.
- `.gitignore`, which ignores everything else.

These are not tracked:

- `index`, which is rebuilt from the messages.
- `hooks/`. Hooks are programs, and running programs that anyone who can push
  to the branch can change would let them run code on the machines of everyone
  who pulls from it.
- `templates/`, which are local customizations of the web interface.
- `config.local`, the local configuration, for settings that differ per clone,
  like the SMTP server or the pager. Its variables override those in `config`.
  `lbts config --local` writes to it.

## Merging never conflicts

Each message is stored in a file named after the hash of its Message-ID, and
messages never change once they are imported. So when two clones add messages,
git merges them without conflicts: each clone only adds new files. Only the
shared configuration can conflict, if two clones change it at the same time.

## Ticket identity

Ticket numbers are assigned in the order tickets are created, but only in one
instance: two clones that create a ticket at the same time give it the same
number. Tickets are therefore identified by an ID, the hash of the Message-ID
of their first message, which is the same everywhere. Messages written by
LightBTS refer to other tickets by their ID, and commands accept a prefix of it.
Numbers remain a local convenience: after a sync, tickets that were created
elsewhere get the next free numbers in that clone.

## Commands

`lbts init --git`, run anywhere in a git repository, sets this up in the top
directory of the repository:

- If the branch `lightbts` exists, locally or as `origin/lightbts`, it is
  checked out in `.lightbts`, and the index is built from its messages. This is
  what a new clone of a repository with tickets needs.
- If `.lightbts` already exists, but is not a worktree, the instance is
  converted: its messages and configuration are moved into a new orphan branch,
  and its local files are kept.
- Otherwise, a new instance is created on a new orphan branch.

With the variable `core.git` set to `true`, which `lbts init --git` does,
LightBTS commits every message it imports to the `lightbts` branch, and
`lbts config` commits changes to the shared configuration. A commit that fails,
for example because git doesn't know the name of the user, is reported, and
the next `lbts sync` commits it.

`lbts sync` synchronizes with the upstream of the `lightbts` branch:

1. It commits any changes that were not committed yet.
2. It runs `git pull --rebase`, so the history stays linear. If that fails,
   because of a conflict in the configuration, it stops, and the conflict has
   to be resolved in `.lightbts` with git.
3. It adds the new messages to the index.
4. It runs `git push`, unless `--no-push` is given. If the branch has no
   upstream yet, it pushes to `origin` and sets the upstream.

## Caveats

- `git clean -ffdx` in the main worktree removes `.lightbts`, including
  messages that were not pushed yet. A single `-f` doesn't, since `.lightbts`
  is a worktree of its own.
- Git commands for the `lightbts` branch run in `.lightbts`, so a remote with
  a relative path, like `../project.git`, refers to another directory than in
  the main worktree. Use an absolute path or a URL for such remotes.
- Since the index is not tracked, a clone that only uses `git pull` in
  `.lightbts`, instead of `lbts sync`, has to run `lbts reindex` afterwards.
